            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Val"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Have been doing them this way for years, The key is par boiling them until just tender before sauteeing then you can do anything with them. My family loves them this way and if we happen to have some wild leeks( in our search for the fiddleheads - (here in the Northeast springtime)then by all means throw a few of them in as well. This dish definitely says \"SPRINGTIME\" for us here in New England and it is SOOOOO Good."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Myrna"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I grew up picking fiddleheads. We boiled them until just tender. I now put them in a pan with just enough water to cover them. I saute them in high quality EVOO and butter, season them with sea salt and pepper. BTW, we ate them raw as well.",
            "datePublished": "2024-05-10T00:06:27.549Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "xcxzxu"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I blanched them for less long and sautéed them a little longer at they were phenomenal. Definitely agree with other comments that mentioned you should add the minced garlic a little later than the fiddleheads.",
            "datePublished": "2021-06-14T01:05:58.897Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "D"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Added shallots and shaved almonds during the saute. It helps to have slightly high heat and add the almonds almost toward the end of the saute so they don't burn and become bitter. Still add the lemon or lime juice at the end in the pan before transferring to serve. Don't forget a small pinch of salt...very needed.",
            "datePublished": "2026-06-28T22:58:59.624Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "RMSR"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Yes, this is a basic recipe that is an excellent introduction to a veggie many people haven't heard of or tried. This was my first time trying these, and I prepped them as described on Health Canada's website (Trim, remove papery husk [if any] and rinse through several changes of water then boil for roughly 8 mins strain and then proceed with recipe.) These cannot be be eaten undercooked or raw otherwise you're putting yourself at risk for food-borne illness. I added some diced onion to this, and think that balsamic vinegar would be a delicious substitute for the lemon juice.",
            "datePublished": "2011-06-05T04:22:59.430Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Ms Jean"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Fiddleheads can be a little intimidating because you don't see them often at the market. I used about half the amount of fiddleheads called for, but kept other amounts the same. The cooking times were spot on for me, simmering for 10 min and sauteeing for 5 min. They were still slightly crisp without being soggy. I used extra garlic and did not add it until there were 2 minutes left in sauteeing, because I was afraid it would burn. The lemon juice was a good addition, adding a nice freshness. Thanks, A Korean, for the simple and delicious spring-like recipe! I enjoyed it!",
            "datePublished": "2014-05-25T14:11:10.563Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Ron Kierstead"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this tonight, as I had all the ingredients to hand. I added some fried yellow onion to the garlic. One commenter suggested that you should boil them minimum time so sautéing them doesn’t turn them mushy. They had it dead to rights. Mine were five and five, lid on at times and I overcooked them a bit, but wonderful!",
            "datePublished": "2025-06-01T21:14:49.230Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Elizabeth Wohler Stowe"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was fantastic. I took the reviewers' suggestion &amp; substituted balsamic vinegar for the lemon juice. My husband, who used to say that he didn't really like fiddleheads, ate a huge helping &amp; loved them.",
            "datePublished": "2020-05-21T11:30:15.193Z"
          },
          {
